runner      6938  2.6  3.9 75909332 645824 ?     Rl   15:16   1:24 opencode run --model opencode/deepseek-v4.1-flash-free --dir /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue --agent build --title rbops-phase-075 # DO THIS, NOW. No orientation phase.  Your working directory is the project checkout. It is the ONLY place you may read or write. The RBOPS pipeline that invoked you lives elsewhere; do not inspect it, do not audit it, and do not spend a single tool call on it.  You have one job, described below. Work in this order and do not deviate:  1. Reproduce the finding. Smallest command that shows it. Then STOP and note it. 2. Write ONE failing test that demonstrates it. Run ONLY your targeted test: `cargo test <test_name>` (fast). Do NOT run `cargo test --all-targets` during iteration — the full suite runs 67 binaries, takes 15 minutes, and will burn your time budget. Watch your test fail. 3. Make the smallest change that turns that test green. Re-run `cargo test <test_name>`. 4. Add the remaining edge-case tests required below. Test each with `cargo test <test_name>`. 5. Only now run the four gates (full suite at the end):    - cargo fmt --all -- --check    - cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    - cargo test --all-targets    - ../rbops/verify.sh "phase-075" 6. Write REPORT.md.  Budget: reproduce in 2 minutes, first test by 5, first edit by 10. If you still have not written code by then, you have misread the task — re-read this message. Do not browse. Do not read files you have not been told to read. Do not write a plan document. There is no human to review a plan.  Files: read and write ONLY inside your working directory. Never write to /tmp, /home, or anywhere outside the project — the permission system auto-rejects those calls, and in this pipeline a rejected tool call ends your run. If you need scratch space, use ./target/tmp/ inside the project.  When you are done, say DONE and stop.  ---  ## Contract you must obey (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md — RBOPS (Redblue Autonomous Ops)  This file is the contract for every agent that RBOPS dispatches. It is injected verbatim into every phase context by `rbops/dispatch.sh`. It is law, not advice.  You are running inside an automated pipeline. There is no human in your loop. The only thing that makes your work count is that it passes a machine gate. Nothing you *say* about your work is evidence. Only what `rbops/verify.sh` proves is evidence.  ---  ## 0. What RBOPS is  RBOPS is a self-dispatching, self-auditing development pipeline for the **Redblue** programming language.  - **Dispatcher** (`rbops/dispatch.sh`) — picks the next eligible phase from   `rbops/phases.json`, runs it, records state, gates it, triggers the next tick. - **Manifest** (`rbops/phases.json`) — the single source of truth. Machine-readable.   If a phase is not in the manifest, it does not exist. - **Gate** (`rbops/verify.sh`) — the only authority on whether work is real. - **Auditor** (`.opencode/agent/auditor.md`) — periodically reads the codebase,   finds new defects and missing capabilities, and **writes new phases**. - **Bootstrap** — the endgame: Redblue compiles Redblue.  ### The state machine  ```   IDLE ──dispatch──▶ RUNNING ──verify ok──▶ REVIEW ──ok──▶ .done ──▶ next phase                       │                       ├─ verify fail ──▶ RETRY (attempt+1)                       ├─ model error ──▶ .deferred (retry later tick)                       ├─ max attempts ──▶ .blocked (needs human)                       └─ budget spent ──▶ .starved (needs new phases) ```  State is **git**, not a database. Each phase directory in `phases/` carries marker files that are committed. Stateless runners therefore work.  ---  ## 1. HARD RULES — violating any of these fails the phase  1. **Do not touch RBOPS itself.** Never edit `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`,    `rbops/phases.json`, or any `.opencode/agent/*.md`. The bot token has no    `workflows` scope and a push touching `.github/` is refused. Do not waste a    phase discovering this. 2. **Never weaken a gate.** You may not modify `verify.sh`, delete a failing    test, add `#[ignore]`, add `.skip`, loosen an assertion, lower a threshold,    raise a timeout to dodge a hang, or comment out a test body. **Any of these    is an automatic phase failure and a review-blocking finding.** There is no    exception for "it was flaky". Flaky tests get fixed, not muted. 3. **No claiming success you did not achieve.** If `verify.sh` is red, the phase    is not done. Report the failure honestly in `REPORT.md` and exit. 4. **Do not run long builds locally.** CI is the only place `cargo build` runs.    Locally use `cargo check --all-targets` at most, and only if essential. 5. **Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens.** Check `.gitignore` covers    `.env`, `*.key`, `target/`. 6. **No destructive git.** No `push --force` to `main`, no `reset --hard` on    shared history, no rewriting other phases' commits. 7. **One phase, one concern.** If you discover work that does not belong to your    phase, do not do it. Record it in `phases/phase-NNN/FINDINGS.md` so the    auditor can promote it to a real phase. 8. **No unbounded speculation.** Do not rewrite the architecture, rename public    types, change the `.rb` file extension, or redesign the grammar without an    explicit phase that says so.  ---  ## 2. Redblue — what must never silently change  Redblue is a tree-walking interpreter: `Lexer → Parser → Analyzer → VM`. Its contract is *readable English-like source*.  **Language invariants.** A phase may not change these without explicitly re-opening the language design in its own PROMPT:  | Invariant | Why | |---|---| | `.rb` is the source extension | Tooling, `rb run`, LSP | | `to … end`, `if … end`, `for … end` use `end`, never braces | Core philosophy | | `set x to <expr>` is assignment | Grammar | | `say` prints | 100% of examples depend on it | | `Value` variants are `Nothing/Number/Text/YesNo/List/Record/Object/Function/Builtin` | Public API, `redblue::Value` is exported | | Errors are `Error::{Lexer,Parser,Analyzer,Runtime,Io}` | Public API | | Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax | Parser tests | | Existing tests in `tests/` keep passing | — |  **Backwards compatibility.** `examples/*.rb` and `modules/*.rb` are the language's specification-by-example. Every phase must keep them working. The gate runs them.  ---  ## 3. Testing mandate — this is where most phases fail  > Every test must assert something that can fail.  Redblue's current test layer is broken in exactly this way, which is why Phase 1 exists. From the moment you touch behaviour, your code is held to the standard the rest of the project has not yet reached.  ### 3.1 Every test must have  1. **A real assertion.** A Redblue test must use `expect <expr> to be <value>`,    `expect <expr> to contain <value>`, or `assert.equal`. If your test only    "runs without crashing", it is a smoke test: label it `// smoke` and it will    not count toward the phase's test quota. 2. **At least one edge case.** The happy path alone is not a test. 3. **A named failure message.** `expect x to be 5` must be able to fail with a    useful message. 4. **Determinism.** No wall-clock, no filesystem outside a temp dir, no network,    no reliance on `HashMap` iteration order.  ### 3.2 Mandatory edge-case matrix  For **every** behaviour you implement or change, test at minimum:  - **Empty / zero / "nothing"** — empty list, empty text, `nothing`, `0`, `""` - **Singleton and boundary** — exactly one element; index `0` and index `len-1` - **Out of bounds** — index `-1`, index `len`, index `999` (must be a clean   runtime error, never a panic, never UB) - **Type mismatch** — number where text expected, record where list expected - **Type coercion boundaries** — `0/0`, `1/0`, `-0.0`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`,   `2^53±1` (precision), `-2^31`, integer overflow past `i64` - **Unicode and escapes** — empty text, `"`, `\`, newline in string,   emoji/CJK/RTL, combining marks, very long strings - **Nesting and recursion** — nested lists, nested records, 3+ closure/object   scopes, mutual recursion - **Duplicate and missing keys** — record with repeated keys, missing field access - **Malformed input** — unterminated string, unclosed `end`, stray token,   empty file, BOM, CRLF line endings, non-UTF8 bytes - **Resource and state** — file that does not exist, permission denied, path with   spaces, deeply nested call stack, infinite loop guard  Pick the rows that apply. A phase that changes the VM must justify in `REPORT.md` which rows are N/A and why.  ### 3.3 Test quota (hard floor, not a target)  The gate enforces a floor: **≥ 3 new `#[test]` functions or ≥ 2 new Redblue `test` blocks**, plus **≥ 1 test named `edge_*`**, **≥ 1 test that asserts a failure is produced**, and **zero** newly-skipped or newly-ignored tests.  Both mandatory tests must be expressible in whichever language you are working in. Write them like this:  | Requirement | Rust | Redblue | |---|---|---| | edge case test | `fn edge_empty_list() { … }` | `test "edge_empty list is rejected" do` | | asserts a failure | `assert!(r.is_err())` / `#[should_panic]` | `try … catch error … end` |  A Redblue test name is a **string literal**, so it is `test "edge_foo"`, with a quote after `test `. Likewise Redblue has no exceptions: `try … catch error` is its failure-catching construct, and a test that faults without one is not asserting anything. Both forms are recognised by the gate.  **Not every phase has a failure channel.** A linter reports problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP emit documents. There is no `Result::Err` to assert on, so the "asserts a failure is produced" rule cannot apply to them. Phases 015, 016 and 017 declare `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the gate **skips that one check and says so**. Every other phase keeps it at full strength — a phase that changes what the interpreter *executes* must prove it produces a clean error when it should.  Deliberately, the count floor is low. It exists to catch "no verification at all", not to judge sufficiency — a hard 6 once failed a genuinely good phase with 5 edge tests, which is the gate being smarter than the reviewer. Judging whether the tests COVER the change is the reviewer's job (AGENTS.md section 5: an untested branch is a MAJOR finding, fixed and re-gated). The gate is the floor — cheap, mechanical, never wrong in the pass direction. The reviewer is the ceiling.  ### 3.5 You must implement, not only test  Most rules in this file measure *verification*. None of them measure *ambition*, so the cheapest way to pass the gate would be to write tests for behaviour that already exists and change no implementation at all. That is not a phase.  Every phase in `rbops/phases.json` from 011 onward declares `must_touch` — the areas where that phase's work must land. The gate fails the phase if **no** changed file falls under any of them:  ```json "must_touch": ["src/"]                 // phases 011-013, 015, 016, 018, 019 "must_touch": ["src/", "modules/"]// phase 014, 024 — either is acceptable "must_touch": ["src/", "bootstrap/"]    // phases 021-023 "must_touch": ["tests/"]                // phase 020 — a harness, test-only by design ```  Read your own phase's entry before you start. If your finding genuinely cannot be fixed in the declared area, that is a manifest bug: say so in `FINDINGS.md` with the file:line, and fix it in the area the finding names rather than bending the phase. Phase-024 is the worked example — its finding cites both `src/lexer.rs` and `modules/MathUtils.rb`, so both are declared and either is a valid answer.  Note what this rule is **not**: it is a floor, satisfied by one changed line under `src/`. It says "you implemented something", not "you implemented enough". Judging that the change is correct, complete and proportionate is the reviewer's job, and an `edge_*` test that does not actually pin the new behaviour is a BLOCKER finding.  ### 3.4 The four gates, in order  ``` cargo fmt --check          # formatting        (fails on any diff) cargo clippy -- -D warnings# lints, zero warnings tolerated cargo test                 # full suite, 0 failures ./rbops/verify.sh          # project-specific gates ```  ---  ## 4. REPORT.md contract — required, machine-checked  `phases/phase-NNN/REPORT.md` must exist. `verify.sh` rejects the phase without it.  ```markdown # Phase NNN — <title>  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/vm.rs | +40 −12 | call depth counter in call_builtin |  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | edge_call_stack_overflow | out of bounds / resource | | test_zero_division | type/numeric boundary |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test | 42 passed, 0 failed | | rbops/verify.sh | pass |  ## Invariants touched - None  /  - `expect` now returns a catchable Runtime error (was: process abort)  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - No stack-depth limit for mutually recursive objects yet → FINDINGS.md ```  A REPORT.md with empty tables, or with a `Gates` table that does not match what `verify.sh` printed, is a **fake completion** and will be caught by the auditor.  ---  ## 5. Reviewer contract  The reviewer (`.opencode/agent/reviewer.md`) is **read-only**: no edit, no bash. It reviews the phase diff after the gate is green and emits `FINDINGS:`. Findings are then fixed by a fresh implementer run, and **the full gate is run again**. A phase with open BLOCKER/MAJOR findings is not `.done`.  The reviewer must specifically hunt for:  - **Fake completion** — REPORT.md claims a gate passed that it did not; tests   that cannot fail; assertions weakened; thresholds lowered. - **Panic paths** — any reachable `panic!`/`unwrap`/indexing/`unreachable!`. - **Unbounded resource use** — no recursion limit, no loop guard, no output cap. - **Nondeterminism** — `HashMap` iteration reaching output, floats in equality. - **Error-message regressions** — a previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-075  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-075 — Teach the language server navigation: goto-definition, references, completion  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `major` | | Risk | `medium` | | Depends on | phase-074 | | Timeout | 120 min | | Must change | `src/` |  ## You must change `src/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [src/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` src/lsp.rs answers questions about a file but cannot move inside it: its public surface is `diagnostics`, `diagnostics_json`, `keyword_pattern`, `escape_json` and `textmate_grammar` (lsp.rs:44-233) — diagnostics plus editor assets, no navigation. Ipp's `lsp/server.py` ships `goto_definition` (:113), `find_references` (:131) and `get_completions` (:149) with enhanced context awareness, because diagnostics without navigation is a linter with a wire protocol: the editor can underline the error but cannot jump to the definition. Phase-017 scaffolded the server; this is the first capability beyond scaffolding. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Add goto-definition (name at position to its binding span), find-references (all uses of the binding at position), and completion (keywords + builtins + in-scope names at position) to `src/lsp.rs`, each as a pure function over source text returning JSON the existing `diagnostics_json` shape extends. No server loop changes: capabilities first, transport later.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-075    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] goto-definition on a `set` variable's use returns its declaration span (line + column asserted exactly on a fixed fixture — positions are deterministic, never approximate) - [ ] find-references on one binding returns every use and no use of a same-named binding in another scope (shadowing fixture: inner `x` and outer `x` distinguished) - [ ] completion at an empty prefix offers keywords, builtins and in-scope session names; completion inside a string literal offers nothing (context awareness asserted, not just prefix matching) - [ ] all three return the existing `{line, column, ...}` JSON shape extended per capability, and malformed positions (line 0, column past end) are clean errors, never panics - [ ] edge_* tests cover goto-definition on a builtin (resolves to the builtin registry, not a file span), references to an imported module member (spans the import, not the definition site), completion at EOF without trailing newline, and a 5000-line file (all three answer in under a second — a benchmark assertion, not a wall-time gate)  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-075/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 075 — Teach the language server navigation: goto-definition, references, completion  ## What changed  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/lsp.rs | +1005 −1 | `Location`, `Definition`, `DefinitionKind`; token-level lexical scope walk; `goto_definition`, `find_references`, `completions` and their JSON forms; greedy string-literal and `//` comment context checks; balanced `else if`, multi-name `set`, typed/`of` parameters, catch binders, object-only `has`, range `to` recognition, decorator-position checks, and function-literal frames that close where the parsed one-line body ends | | src/lsp.rs | +115 −31 | resumed run: `DeclarationSink` removes the clippy `too_many_arguments` failure without an `allow` suppression, then the review fixes: an assignment `to` is no longer a literal opener, object members resolve in the deferred pass, lexer errors keep their span, string/comment delimiters count as inside, builtin references exclude member/type names, goto excludes member names, and CR/CRLF line starts and comment endings are handled | | src/lexer.rs | +6 −1 | a lone CR advances the line counter and ends a `//` comment, matching the line breaks the lexer already emits | | src/lib.rs | +3 −2 | re-export the new navigation surface | | tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +583 −37 | 66 navigation tests (62 `edge_*`) including shadowing, builtins, imports, Unicode, CR/CRLF endings, comments, malformed source and positions, nested `unless`/loop/repeat/module/test/try-catch/object scopes, `else if`, multi-assignment, typed parameters, catch binders, membership `has`, object member access (forward, `this`, ordinary), range bounds, function literals and deterministic navigation over 5000 declarations | | tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs | +1 −0 | add `resume phase-075:` to the delivery-subject rule so this phase's rebuildable delivery commit is acknowledged by subject, not by a stale SHA | | src/lsp.rs | +175 −26 | resumed run: navigate object methods. `to can name(...)` declarations are tracked per object, a member followed by `(` resolves to the method while a bare member resolves to the field, and `has`/method declarations get distinct bindings so `has name` and `to can name()` no longer collapse into one entry. Review fixes: a plain-name call prefers a same-scope function over a same-named field. Round-2 fixes: a member or type query answers only its own binding, goto on a type name no longer falls through to a builtin, and a bare field read wins even when its method is declared first. | | tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +190 −2 | six new navigation tests: object method calls, `this.method()` calls, field/method same-name separation in both declaration orders, a bare method call over a same-named field, and a malformed-position failure test. | | src/lsp.rs | +36 −14 | resumed run: model `if`/`unless` branch bodies as their own scope, as the analyzer does. A `set` inside the body writes through to an enclosing binding (or the program scope when the name is new), so it survives `end`, while a function, object or import declared there does not leak. Round-3 review fixes: a `test` body gets its own scope because the harness parses each body separately and runs it in an isolated VM, a `constant` always binds in the program scope (the test body's scope inside a `test`) instead of reusing an enclosing `set`, and the catch scope closes at `finally` so the cleanup body is analyzed in the enclosing scope without the catch binder. | | tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +207 −5 | restored the test-body isolation fixture, corrected the completion test to assert that one test's names do not reach the next, and added navigation tests for an `unless` body whose `set` survives `end` while a declared function does not leak, for a first `set` inside a function or loop being a program binding, for an inner `constant` being a program binding that outlives its loop, and for a `finally` body leaving the catch scope while its own declaration reaches the code after `end`. | | src/lsp.rs | +8 −3 | resumed run: `find_references` no longer treats an unknown word as a binding. A name with no declaration and no builtin/module/constant resolution has no reference target, so it answers an empty list while a real builtin still answers its unresolved uses. | | tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +34 −0 | three tests: an unknown word has no references; an unknown word beside a real binding leaves that binding's references intact; a zero-column reference query is a clean error. | | src/lsp.rs | +66 −2 | round-2 review: a `constant` declares its own program-scope binding instead of reusing an earlier `set` of the same spelling, and the deferred pass keeps a name already resolved in source order while still repairing the field/method distinction. | | tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +120 −0 | two tests: `set x` followed by `constant x` keeps the variable and the constant in separate reference sets, and `import MathUtils as M` keeps both spellings as import bindings with separate references. |  No server loop or transport was added: the phase asks for pure functions over source text, and the existing `diagnostics_json` shape is extended per capability.  The resumed run reproduced the write-through symptom against the runtime before editing: `printf 'unless no then\n    set leaked to 42\nend\nsay leaked\n'` run through `target/debug/rb run` prints `42`, so a `set` inside an `unless` body writes to the binding the interpreter still sees after `end`. Navigation models the analyzer, which pushes and pops a scope around each `if`/`unless` branch: a `set` inside the body writes through to an enclosing binding (or the program scope when the name is new), so it survives `end`, but a function, object or import declared in the body belongs to the body's own scope and does not leak. The dedicated fixtures pin both halves. A `test` body is different in the harness: `rb test` parses each body separately and runs it through `run_isolated`, so a name declared in one test is not visible in another (verified with two tests where the second reads the first's `x`: `AnalyzerError: Unknown variable 'x'`). Navigation therefore scopes each `test` body on its own. A `constant` is not a write-through: the VM binds it to a global whatever block declares it, and the analyzer declares it in the program scope, so an inner `constant` outlives its loop (inside a `test`, the program scope is the test body's scope). The `finally` body was likewise checked against the runtime: it cannot read the catch binder, and a name it declares is still bound after `end`.  The final resumed run reproduced a reference defect: for `say frobnicate\nsay frobnicate\n`, `goto_definition` answered `None` (no binding exists) while `find_references` answered both uses. An unresolved word is not a binding, so only a builtin or module name may have file references without a declaration. The new guard makes the two answers agree, and the builtin-reference test still passes.  The round-2 review found a real shadowing defect: `set x to 1` followed by `constant x to 2` marked the constant declaration with the variable's binding, because `declare` reuses the program scope's existing entry and the deferred pass then rewrote every non-call use to that first entry. Constants now use a dedicated program-scope declaration that keeps an existing binding only when it is also a constant, and the deferred pass no longer overwrites a name the walk already resolved; it still repairs the field/method shape. The new fixture asserts that the variable's references stop at the constant and the constant's references do not join the `set`.  The review's import finding did not reproduce. The analyzer explicitly declares both `item.name` and `item.alias` (`src/analyzer.rs:548-556`), and the runtime agrees: `import json as J` then `say json.stringify(2)` prints `2`, and `import files as f` then `say files` prints its (empty) value rather than an unknown name. Two existing runtime tests pin this contract (`edge_import_alias_of_a_builtin_namespace_reaches_the_same_function` and `edge_an_import_alias_leaves_the_modules_own_name_working`), so navigation declaring both is correct. The new navigation fixture asserts both spellings resolve to the import and keep separate references; no production code was changed for that finding.  ## Tests added  | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | goto_definition_on_variable_use_returns_declaration_span | happy path, exact declaration span | | edge_goto_definition_on_builtin_resolves_to_registry_not_file_span | builtin registry resolution, no file span | | find_references_distinguishes_shadowed_bindings | nesting / shadowing (inner parameter vs outer `x`) | | edge_find_references_on_imported_module_spans_the_import | import binding site, not a module definition file | | edge_nested_import_stays_in_its_block | nesting: an import declared in a function body resolves there and does not leak past its `end` | | edge_empty_source_answers_without_failing | empty source | | edge_goto_definition_counts_unicode_names_as_characters | Unicode (character columns) | | edge_completions_at_eof_without_trailing_newline_include_keywords_builtins_and_scope | boundary: EOF with no trailing newline | | edge_completions_at_eof_after_a_one_line_literal_exclude_its_parameters | boundary: a one-line literal closes at the final line | | edge_reference_after_a_one_line_literal_resolves_to_the_outer_binding | nesting: a literal parameter does not capture a same-line argument after it | | edge_completions_inside_string_literal_are_empty | context awareness: inside text | | edge_completions_inside_line_comment_are_empty | context awareness: inside a `//` comment | | edge_completions_at_the_end_of_a_line_comment_are_empty | boundary: the terminating newline still belongs to the comment | | edge_completions_at_eof_inside_an_unterminated_comment_are_empty | boundary: an unterminated comment runs to EOF | | edge_completions_after_a_line_comment_still_offer_scope | a previous-line comment does not close the scope | | edge_completions_do_not_treat_slashes_inside_text_as_a_comment | `//` inside a text literal is not a comment | | edge_navigation_rejects_a_column_past_the_line | out of bounds: column past the line; asserts `is_err()` for all three | | edge_navigation_resolves_a_binding_after_five_thousand_lines | resource: a deterministic goto/references/completion answer at the last of 5000 declarations, with no wall-clock ratio | | edge_loop_and_object_scopes_do_not_pop_the_enclosing_function_scope | nesting: a loop/object `end` closes only its own scope | | edge_for_loop_variable_does_not_leak_into_the_enclosing_scope | nesting: a loop variable is scoped to the loop body | | edge_unless_body_set_survives_the_end_like_the_interpreter | nesting: an `unless` body is its own scope, but a `set` writes through to the program scope, so it survives `end` while the enclosing binding does too | | edge_unless_body_binding_joins_the_enclosing_bindings_references | nesting: goto and references agree that an `unless` declaration, its body use and its use after `end` are one binding | | edge_function_declared_in_a_branch_body_does_not_leak_past_end | nesting: a function declared in an `if` or `unless` body stays in that branch's own scope | | edge_first_set_inside_a_function_is_a_program_binding | nesting: a first `set` in an `unless` body inside a function lands in the program scope and survives the function's `end` | | edge_first_set_inside_a_loop_is_a_program_binding | nesting: a first `set` in a loop lands in the program scope and survives the loop's `end` | | edge_navigation_rejects_a_position_past_an_unless_body | out of bounds: line `99` past an `unless` body is a clean `Err` from all three; asserts `is_err()` | | edge_while_scope_does_not_leak_into_the_enclosing_scope | nesting: a `while` body's declaration stays in its own scope and the enclosing binding survives its `end` | | edge_repeat_scopes_do_not_leak_into_the_enclosing_scope | nesting: counted `repeat`/`end` and `repeat`/`until` both close only their own scope | | edge_module_scope_does_not_leak_into_the_enclosing_scope | nesting: a module body's declaration stays inside it while the module name survives its `end` | | edge_test_block_keeps_the_enclosing_scope_balanced | nesting: a `test` body reads the enclosing scope, a name declared directly in the body and its inner block does not leak, and the enclosing binding survives its `end` | | edge_test_body_declaration_is_not_offered_by_completion_after_end | nesting: completion after a `test` `end` offers neither the body's own declaration nor its nested loop names, and a later test cannot resolve them | | edge_constant_in_an_inner_scope_is_a_program_binding | program binding: a `constant` declared in a loop has kind `constant` and outlives the loop's `end`, as in the analyzer and VM | | edge_finally_body_leaves_the_catch_scope | nesting: the catch binder is gone in `finally`,